       1   00:4000                      ; ESPUNAPI TCP/IP SSH UNAPI BIOS v.1.0
       2   00:4000                      ; Oduvaldo Pavan Junior
       3   00:4000                      ; ducasp@gmail.com
       4   00:4000                      ;    This code implements TCP/IP and SSH UNAPI SpecificationM
       5   00:4000                      ;
       6   00:4000                      ; Pieces of this code were based on DENYOTCP.ASM (Denyonet ROM)
       7   00:4000                      ; made by Konamiman
       8   00:4000                      ;
       9   00:4000                      ; This code also has contributions from KdL, most specifically in the Wi-Fi
      10   00:4000                      ; setup menus and functionality, he has helped quite a lot to get it to the
      11   00:4000                      ; final format and functionality, thanks!
      12   00:4000                      ;
      13   00:4000                      ; This code has contribution from Jeroen Taverne that helped making some
      14   00:4000                      ; MACROs on the fixed / non speed optimized loop I/O parts of the code so
      15   00:4000                      ; we can easily switch this from I/O based to Memory Based.
      16   00:4000                      ;
      17   00:4000                      ; Note: this implementation depends upon ESP having the UNAPI
      18   00:4000                      ; firmware flashed. This firmware has been developed by me as well.
      19   00:4000                      ;
      20   00:4000                      ; Usage is free as long as you publish your code changes. If you make a profit
      21   00:4000                      ; selling something using this code, a suggestion is that you donate one or two
      22   00:4000                      ; pieces of the hardware to the MSX DEV contest to be awarded to MSX DEV winners
      23   00:4000                      ; as this shouldn't cost much and perhaps will bring interest on developers to
      24   00:4000                      ; make games that support internet :)
      25   00:4000                      ;
      26   00:4000                      ; Design details:
      27   00:4000                      ; There are five instances of this ROM
      28   00:4000                      ;
      29   00:4000                      ; 1 - If configured as disabled in set-up menu item 4, or through setting F2
      30   00:4000                      ;     device to EF or keeping DEL pressed during boot, it won't load at all.
      31   00:4000                      ; 2 - F1 is pressed during boot, or through setting F2 device to F1, it will
      32   00:4000                      ;     show a setup menu, ESC resume boot.
      33   00:4000                      ; 3 - F2 is pressed during a soft reset, or trhough setting F2 device to F2 it
      34   00:4000                      ;     will force re-set of the ESP and re-connection to the AP.
      35   00:4000                      ; 4 - No key is pressed during a warm boot and F2 device is set to F0, UNAPI 
      36   00:4000                      ;     is enabled (default), EXTBIO HOOK for UNAPI calls is installed and HTIM_I
      37   00:4000                      ;     hook for internal usage and a few bytes at top of TPA are reserved upon
      38   00:4000                      ;     the first UNAPI function being called. No ESP reset or tries to set-up the
      39   00:4000                      ;     the RTC are done, so it is a quick warm-boot.
      40   00:4000                      ; 5 - No key is pressed during a cold boot or F2 is set to FE or any unknown
      41   00:4000                      ;     value, UNAPI is enabled (default), EXTBIO HOOK for UNAPI calls is installed
      42   00:4000                      ;     and HTIM_I hook for internal usage and a few bytes at top of TPA are reserved
      43   00:4000                      ;     upon the first UNAPI function being called. ESP is reset and if BIOS is set
      44   00:4000                      ;     up to do so, SNTP connection attempt is made and RTC is updated
      45   00:4000                      ;
      46   00:4000                      ; As a ROM, our segment is read only, so for RAM needs we need to use RAM not
      47   00:4000                      ; in our page. This ROM will do the following:
      48   00:4000                      ;
      49   00:4000                      ; If running the SETUP menu, we are going to "borrow" BASIC program area, that
      50   00:4000                      ; is saved at TXTTAB. We can't allocate memory at this point as any possible
      51   00:4000                      ; disk rom might be sitting on a slot that has not been initialized and results
      52   00:4000                      ; could be bad. The BASIC program area trick should be safe, unless some other
      53   00:4000                      ; cartridge runs a BASIC software for something (i.e.: wait disks be initialized
      54   00:4000                      ; ), in such case, that BASIC software or trick will most likely be corrupted
      55   00:4000                      ; after running our setup.
      56   00:4000                      ;
      57   00:4000                      ; After boot, we need RAM in the 4th page. We have the SLOT WORK AREA for free,
      58   00:4000                      ; it is 8 bytes reserved for our slot, and that is certainly not enough, just
      59   00:4000                      ; the EXTBIOS HOOK backup cost us 5 bytes, so we have three bytes remaining. Of
      60   00:4000                      ; those, we use two to indicate the bottom of the memory reserved in the 4th
      61   00:4000                      ; page. Whenever more memory is needed, HIMEM_RESERVED_SIZE must change, just
      62   00:4000                      ; remember that more memory reserved means less memory available to
      63   00:4000                      ; applications and BASIC, so use it wisely.
      64   00:4000                      ;
      65   00:4000                      ; Slot Work Area Details
      66   00:4000                      ; 0000 - 0004   : EXTBIO hook backup
      67   00:4000                      ; 0005 - 0006   : HIMEM Allocated area (starts with 00 xx, once allocated goes
      68   00:4000                      ;                 to Fxxx or Exxx so checking 0005 if 00 is enough)
      69   00:4000                      ; 007           : Flags: bit 0 ESP32, bit 1 SSH supported, bit 7 probed
      70   00:4000                      ;
      71   00:4000                      ; Current HIMEM mapping offset related to the address stored in the 6th and 7th
      72   00:4000                      ; bytes of our slot work area:
      73   00:4000                      ;
      74   00:4000                      ; 0000 - 0004   : HTIM_I hook backup
      75   00:4000                      ; 0005 - 0006   : Counter that our HTIM_I hook updates
      76   00:4000                      ; 0007          : Store Single Byte some functions need
      77   00:4000                      ; 0008          : Stores whether DNS is ready or not
      78   00:4000                      ; 0009 - 000A   : Stores last DNS result
      79   00:4000                      ; 000B - 000C   : Stores a 16 bits value
      80   00:4000                      ; 000D - 0012   : Store a backup of BC / DE / HL
      81   00:4000                      ;   000D - 000E : C and B
      82   00:4000                      ;   000F - 0010 : D and E
      83   00:4000                      ;   0011 - 0012 : H and L
      84   00:4000                      ; 0014 - 001D   : Unused
      85   00:4000                      ;
      86   00:4000                      ; As this is sitting on a slot, there is a resoanable chance that this is initialized
      87   00:4000                      ; before any disk controllers. If that is the case, allocating memory at HIMEM is bad
      88   00:4000                      ; and might have two bad behaviors:
      89   00:4000                      ;
      90   00:4000                      ; 1 - Disk Controller is smart enough to determine HIMEM was moved and simply do not
      91   00:4000                      ; initialize. No disk available...
      92   00:4000                      ; 2 - Disk Controller doesn't care and simply wipe-out memory below original HIMEM at
      93   00:4000                      ; boot to create the disk interfaces static work area, thus, causing our allocated
      94   00:4000                      ; memory to be overwritten and MSX will most likely crash on ther first VDP interrupt
      95   00:4000                      ; after that...
      96   00:4000                      ;
      97   00:4000                      ; So, allocating memory at cartridge startup will make MSX freeze or misbehave or not
      98   00:4000                      ; have the disk available if our ROM is sitting in a slot lower than the disk. There
      99   00:4000                      ; are a few possibilities:
     100   00:4000                      ;
     101   00:4000                      ; 1 - Hook to H_STKE, it is called once all slots have been initialized... But, using
     102   00:4000                      ; this hook will make ESE SCSI to not work and halt with a "No enough memory" message,
     103   00:4000                      ; so I do not consider it a good option as the intention is to re-use code for other
     104   00:4000                      ; adapters.
     105   00:4000                      ; 2 - On any UNAPI call check the slot work area if the allocated area is other than 00
     106   00:4000                      ; and if it is, execute, otherwise, first execute our allocation routine. This has an
     107   00:4000                      ; overhead of a dozen instructions at every UNAPI function we execute, does not seem to
     108   00:4000                      ; impact performance compared to the UNAPI RAM driver that doesn't has it
     109   00:4000                      ; 3 - Try to go crazy and check for dos roms on slots above us, initialize those first
     110   00:4000                      ; if found... Too much trouble for a dozen instructions.
     111   00:4000                      ;
     112   00:4000                      ; So, for the moment the design decision is option #2
     113   00:4000                      ;
     114   00:4000                      ; F2 Device by KdL
     115   00:4000                      ; That device, present on recent OCM implementations, allow a byte to be saved and
     116   00:4000                      ; read from IO address F2. It has FF on start-up, and KdL devised the following
     117   00:4000                      ; values/states:
     118   00:4000                      ;
     119   00:4000                      ; FF - Default, meaning cold boot
     120   00:4000                      ; FE - Set this when an unexpected error occurred
     121   00:4000                      ; F2 - Equivalent to keeping F2 pressed at boot time, force the next warm boot
     122   00:4000                      ;      to try to detect the ESP8266
     123   00:4000                      ; F1 - Will force the Wi-Fi setup at next warm boot, so it is not needed to
     124   00:4000                      ;      keep F1 pressed
     125   00:4000                      ; F0 - Means that RTC has date and time, so the next warm boot doesn't check
     126   00:4000                      ;      for F1/F2 or try to update RTC, being faster
     127   00:4000                      ; EF - Set when ESP was not found or by user at runtime to disable UNAPI
     128   00:4000                      ;      next warm boot
     129   00:4000                      ;
     130   00:4000                      ; MSX Pico also implements the F2 device
     131   00:4000                      
     132   00:4000                      ;*************************
